Fleet task substrate: dispatch, review, and git-observed landing

Usage: cv task <COMMAND>

Commands:
  open       Open a new task. Prints its id
  list       List tasks: non-terminal, touched in the last 14 days or involving you ($CV_ENDPOINT). The hidden count is the last line; `--all` lifts both the window and the terminal filter
  show       Show one task (id may be a unique prefix)
  claim      Claim an open task (first writer wins)
  release    Release a claim back to open
  note       Record a progress note. `cv task note <id> -` reads the note from stdin
  done       Complete a task. Self-reported and labeled so: `--observed` is free text cv records, never a claim cv checked. Refused on a decision (answer it with `resolve`)
  status     Pin the task's current STATUS (replaces the previous one; history stays in the events): the relay hand-off — tip, what is merged, what runs, what is next. `show` prints it first. By the task's assignee or opener; refused on a terminal task
  abandon    Kill a task
  supersede  Mark a task superseded by another
  tag        Add tags to a task (comma-separated; additive, never removes)
  block      Record that a task waits on another: `cv task block <id> --by <blocker>`
  decide     Pose a decision for someone: a task of kind `decision` with a default that stands if they stay silent, the alternatives, and an optional deadline. Prints its id
  resolve    Answer a decision: `--accept-default`, or `--choice "<option>"` (one of the posed options). Records WHO resolved it: pass `--from <you>` or set `CV_ENDPOINT` once. On a decision made for you provisionally, `--confirm` keeps its choice and `--choice` vetoes it
  events     New task events since a point in time, one JSON line each (the poll surface: hand the last event id back as the next `--since`). `--text` renders one human line per event
  sweep      Candidates for closing, from what git and the filesystem say — never closes anything. Lists non-terminal tasks whose `--issue` path no longer exists, or whose title/body names a branch now merged into the repo's main (`git branch --merged`)
  inbox      What needs `<who>`: decisions owed (default + options on one line each), decisions made for them provisionally, then assigned actions and claimed work. Same 14-day window as `list`
  help       Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help  Print help
